A non-canonical selector is printed from its canonical text, with `&`
replaced by the class, also inside `:not()` and `:has()`.

  $ cat > m.ml <<EOF
  > let m = [%css "&:not(&:FOCUS):hover { color: red; }"]
  > EOF
  $ cat > n.ml <<EOF
  > let n = [%css "&:has(+ &:HOVER):FOCUS { color: red; }"]
  > EOF
  $ for x in m n; do ../../standalone.exe --env production --impl $x.ml -o out_$x.ml; done
  $ styled-ppx.generate out_m.ml out_n.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._c7fVR-xdlG8T1c1:not(._c7fVR-xdlG8T1c1:focus):hover{color:red;}._c7fYbrzxikoxBZr:has(+ ._c7fYbrzxikoxBZr:hover):focus{color:red;}

Spaces inside `selector()` can be a descendant combinator: `a :hover` is
a hovered descendant of `a`, `a:hover` is a hovered `a`. The argument
keeps its spaces, with runs collapsed to one.

  $ cat > o.ml <<EOF
  > let o = [%css "@supports selector(a  :hover) { color: red; }"]
  > EOF
  $ cat > p.ml <<EOF
  > let p = [%css "@supports selector(a:hover) { color: red; }"]
  > EOF
  $ for x in o p; do ../../standalone.exe --env production --impl $x.ml -o out_$x.ml; done
  $ styled-ppx.generate out_o.ml out_p.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @supports selector(a :hover){._c7f0uJKoId_t5fB{color:red;}}@supports selector(a:hover){._c7f8DdapXQKmL4s{color:red;}}
